#!/usr/bin/env node
/** EH001 siman 001 editorial patch — 15 MT-garbage blocks. */
import { patchFile } from "./_patch-siman-utils.mjs";

const REL = {
  baer: "siman_001/baer-hetev/part-001.txt",
  beer: "siman_001/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt",
  meir: "siman_001/beit-meir/part-001.txt",
  shmuel: "siman_001/beit-shmuel/part-001.txt",
  gra: "siman_001/beur-hagra/part-001.txt",
  chokhmat: "siman_001/chokhmat-shlomo/part-001.txt",
  pitchei: "siman_001/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt",
  tz: "siman_001/turei-zahav/part-001.txt",
};

patchFile(REL.baer, "baer-hetev", {
  "7#_": `**And he converted.** In the responsum of Mahari Segal siman 223 he ruled that even if his sons did not convert with him he fulfills peru u-revu. And so it appears from Tosefta Yevamot 62a in the passage beginning "Rabbi Yose says: he fulfills peru u-revu," etc. And Kenesset HaGedolah ruled that gentile children do not count toward fulfillment of peru u-revu — see there. And see what Beit Yosef wrote. And see what the Tosefta in Chagigah 2b wrote in the passage beginning "He did not create it to be desolate," etc., where they conclude "and furthermore, peru u-revu is written regarding all the children of Noah, even regarding Canaan" — see there. And this is against the sugya in chapter four of Mitzot where they say peru u-revu was stated for Israel and not for the children of Noah — see there. And so too Marhasha, and he left it undecided. And in the responsum of Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 134 he was asked about this and answered that the Tosafot in Chagigah wrote this as an answer for the one who holds that the children of Noah are commanded even regarding castration, according to Rabbi Chidka who said so in chapter four of Mitzot. And do not wonder at this, for Semag also ruled thus, and so too Tur Hashem siman 259, whose words are brought in Beit Yosef seif 6 [5]. And we must say that although it is straightforward in the Gemara to say that the mitzvah of peru u-revu was not stated for the children of Noah — that is, they are not compelled to fulfill the mitzvah of peru u-revu — nevertheless, the permission is in their hands not to engage in peru u-revu at all; but to castrate oneself in a matter where it is impossible for them to engage in peru u-revu — this certainly when forbidden is fulfilled according to Rabbi Chidka. And if so, regarding this half-slave half-freedman who is literally like castration, who cannot marry a maidservant nor a free woman, they properly wrote Tosafot that regarding this matter even the children of Noah are commanded — end quote. And I heard to answer according to Tosafot in Yevamot 62a in the passage beginning "sons of peru u-revu," etc.: "But you will ask — from chapter four of Mitzot it seems that gentiles are not commanded regarding peru u-revu." And one could answer that here he does not speak of those obligated in peru u-revu, but rather that his sons are called by his name — see there. Accordingly, one can explain what Tosafot wrote in Chagigah that "peru u-revu is written regarding all the children of Noah" — that is, that his sons are called by his name, and from this that they are relevant to peru u-revu even though they are not obligated. Thus I heard, but I did not understand it, for the Tosefta concludes "even regarding Canaan," and there they say that everyone agrees regarding a slave who has no lineage — see and consider.`,

  "10#ב": `**To marry.** So too Maharik root 101. And see Maharik root 63 who wrote regarding a betrothed woman who claims "he is repugnant to me," etc., to permit him to divorce against his will or marry another — see there; and that responsum of Maharik the Rama ruled in accordance with him in siman 76 seif 2 in the gloss — see there; from which it appears that even for a betrothed woman he needs permission on grounds of rebellious wife, and he does not say that for a betrothed woman Rabbenu Gershom did not decree at all. And it seems to me that what they say — that for a betrothed woman Rabbenu Gershom did not impose the cherem — means specifically if he does not wish to consummate the marriage but only to release her; but if his intention is to consummate with her eventually, anyone who stands ready to consummate is considered as consummated; immediately the cherem applies to him not to marry another upon his wife even while she is still betrothed. Therefore in root 63, where he wishes to consummate with her when she is rebellious against him, therefore the cherem applies to him and he needs permission on grounds of rebellious wife. And this is the explanation in the gloss that writes on this "and similarly for a betrothed woman if he does not wish to consummate but only to release" — Shevut Yaakov part 1 siman 52; and in the responsum of Chacham Tzvi question 124 he wrote that the Rama's explanation is what he wrote "if he does not wish to consummate but only to release" — that she should not err that if he has a betrothed woman he is permitted to marry another upon her even though he intends to consummate his betrothal; and on the grounds that the second is not forbidden since the first is not yet his wife; and also the first would be permitted to marry another after the second's marriage, since with permission she was betrothed to him — he comes to teach that it is forbidden. And in Beit Yosef he explained the Rama stamma — see there. And see Beit Shmuel. And in the body of the law Chacham Tzvi in his responsum siman 124 disagrees and rules that for a betrothed woman too Rabbenu Gershom imposed the cherem — see there. And so too in the responsum of Meimonit siman 34 and Mahari Segal seif 1; but in the responsum of Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 112 and in Choshen Mishpat siman 52 he ruled like Maharik that for a betrothed woman he did not impose the cherem, and so too Meiri part 2 siman 209, and Maharshadam chiddushei aggadah siman 78. Ben Asher betrothed one virgin and one Hebrew maidservant in his house before witnesses, and afterward he regretted it; also his father and family members protested not to bring her into marriage; and the virgin does not want to accept her get; and he wants to marry another — should he be prevented on account of the cherem? Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 112 ruled that he should not be prevented on account of the cherem, for for a betrothed woman he did not impose the cherem. And according to the words of Chacham Tzvi above who disagrees and rules that for a betrothed woman too Rabbenu Gershom imposed the cherem — he should be prevented: and so Kenesset HaGedolah in the gloss on Beit Yosef seif 59 raised that Maharik speaks when the first is in betrothal, but when the first is in marriage, even though the second is in betrothal, he violates the cherem — unlike Maharshadam chiddushei aggadah siman 78 who holds that whenever the second is not in marriage but only in betrothal he does not violate the cherem — see there. And see the responsum of Beit Yosef siman 12 regarding laws of gittin, and Maharit chiddushei aggadah siman 8, and Maran haLevi siman 21: a minor for whom a yevamah fell, and while he was still a minor he became engaged to his match, and now he is thirteen years and one day and has not brought two hairs, and the time of marriage has arrived; and the girl's father wants to marry his daughter to him as per the engagement conditions — should he be prevented from marrying his match on account of the cherem even though it is written regarding yevamah that he did not impose the cherem — that is, when he wishes to perform yibbum upon his married wife, not so when he wishes to marry his bound yevamah? Chacham Tzvi question 16 ruled that he is permitted to marry his match before he performs chalitzah for the yevamah, and there is no issue of cherem here; and even according to the Rama who ruled that one acts in a place of mitzvah, and even in a place of yibbum he must perform chalitzah — that is, that he can perform chalitzah, but not forced as here — see there. And see siman 104.`,
});

patchFile(REL.beer, "beer-hagolah", {
  "10#ג": `Response of Rashba.`,
  "11#_": `Response of Rosh, general 43, section 7.`,
});

patchFile(REL.meir, "beit-meir", {
  "2#_": `But before thirteen he should not marry, for it is like prostitution — Beit Yosef explained: because the Sages did not enact marriage for a minor; therefore Beit Shmuel brings the view of Tosafot and Rosh who wrote that it is not prostitution since through kiddushin she is bound to him. This language — "since through kiddushin she is bound to him" — is said only according to Rosh chapter 2 of Kiddushin; but Tosafot in chapter haNisrafim and chapter haIsha Rabba wrote in this language "nevertheless Rabbi Yose says there is no prohibition, for it is not intercourse of prostitution." And one could say their reason: they follow Ramban in siman 26 in Beit Shmuel seif 2, that for a designated woman there is no prohibition at all even without kiddushin; and marriage of a minor is no worse than a designated woman. (And according to the flow of the argument there is proof that at least for a pilegesh there is no Torah prohibition, from what they enacted marriage for a minor orphan girl; and even according to Rambam that anything without kiddushin has a positive prohibition of "when he takes," etc. — according to his words even for a designated woman, i.e. pilegesh, this prohibition applies — how can they enact for him to transgress a positive commandment, even a positive commandment? We conclude in Yevamot chapter 9 specifically for "sit and do not act"; but for Rambam one must interpret that marriage of a minor is considered a matter in which the Sages have power to uproot even a Torah matter, as Tosafot conclude there in the passage beginning "since," etc. — see their words. And one opinion of Tosafot in the passage beginning "they immerse.") And one could also say the intent of Tosafot is as Beit Shmuel understood: "since nevertheless," etc. However, in chapter haBa al Yevimto 62b they explained their intent is not according to this, for they wrote "nevertheless for this reason they enacted marriage for him, that his intercourse not be prostitution" — it appears from their language that specifically since through kiddushin and marriage she is bound to him, because of the mitzvah to marry near her maturity they needed to enact this rabbinic marriage so that his intercourse not be prostitution. And necessarily they hold there is prohibition at least for pilegesh; and it also appears from their words that before near her maturity, i.e. the year before majority, in this there is no mitzvah and there is also prohibition because of "lest she become pregnant"; and from the Torah they would enact marriage for him — if so, besides the prohibition of "lest she become pregnant," prostitution also applies. And a practical difference: one who marries a minor Kutite — because of "lest she become pregnant" there is no concern since she too is saturated; but because of prostitution there is concern. And even though we establish that a minor who eats nevelah — bet din is not commanded to separate him — nevertheless regarding those who endeavor, the prohibition applies not to marry him off. And behold, according to Tosafot in chapter haNisrafim and chapter haIsha Rabba one could say the default follows their explanation in chapter haBa al Yevimto, and not as Beit Shmuel's wording implies; but in Rosh one cannot explain it this way — "for this reason they enacted marriage for a minor that his intercourse not be prostitution" — for if so, how does he derive from this marriage of a minor during his father's lifetime? Even though the Torah did not give the Sages power over marriage for the matter of refusal, nevertheless he is not obligated to separate them and it is not prostitution. Or Rosh's plain intent is that without the Sages' enactment it would not be prostitution; nevertheless, from his precision "since through kiddushin," etc., we learn he holds that anything without kiddushin is forbidden even for pilegesh. And this first chapter of Kiddushin that Tosafot brought — Beit Shmuel wrote accordingly that it is possible to fulfill peru u-revu without kiddushin through pilegesh; and necessarily one must interpret according to Ramban's words in Beit Shmuel above on the difficulty from the verse "a bride without blessing is forbidden," etc. — see Tosafot there. And one could say similarly that even though he holds pilegesh is permitted, nevertheless regarding a minor and minor girl marrying for acquisition it is inferior and relevant is what the Torah said — in this manner there must be kiddushin and blessing; and kiddushin for a minor and minor girl is prostitution. And necessarily it is not prostitution since through kiddushin, etc. If so, one could say he holds specifically an adult pilegesh fit to bear children whose intent is to fulfill peru u-revu is permitted; but a minor and minor girl who are not fit for peru u-revu except through kiddushin — they are prostitution. And it is clear according to the aforementioned Ramban in Beit Shmuel that even Rambam among those who permit pilegesh, and also according to the later authorities there on the Tur that they too hold thus. Here, according to Beit Yosef's explanation of Rambam and Tur, that before thirteen years and one day it is prostitution because the Torah did not give the Sages power over marriage — therefore one must also interpret in one of the two manners above. And nevertheless Beit Shmuel's difficulty remains, that the Tur would contradict his father's view. And in my view there is a greater difficulty: he should have explained that it is forbidden to marry him off before thirteen because of "lest she become pregnant," regarding which everyone agrees. (Because it is more than a year before majority.) And further difficulty according to Beit Yosef's explanation: the prohibition emerges from the fact that the Sages did not enact marriage for him; and if so, one must explain what they answer "near her maturity" — "this is different," i.e. after thirteen years and one day, and not as one might think while he is still a minor. If so, the questioner — what is the case of kiddushin for him on Rav's statement that applies only to minor and adult? One could answer that the mitzvah is for minor and minor girl. He should rather have asked from the braita that it is prostitution, and answer that "near her maturity" means after thirteen years and one day; and we could say the questioner too understood the braita as after thirteen years and one day; nevertheless he thought "lest she become pregnant" applies — this is strained. And further, in any case there is difficulty on Rav who said because of "lest she become pregnant," and the verse that it is prostitution. And further great difficulty: if so, even after thirteen years and one day he should require examination for marriage by hairs, for kiddushin depends on hairs as explained in Darkei Moshe siman 43 — how is it a mitzvah near her maturity even after thirteen? And necessarily we rely on Rava's presumption in Niddah. However, in Darkei Moshe he asked on Beit Yosef: from where did he see to explain Rambam's language unlike Rashi? In my view, prostitution mentioned in their words is strained — they hold unlike Rashi; and further, since they hold prostitution applies to a minor boy, there cannot be mitzvah in the year before majority — as Tosafot's difficulty; therefore they explained according to the Tur that there is mitzvah only after thirteen years and one day, when we rely on Rava's presumption. However, from Hagahot Maimoniyot it appears he explains Rambam literally from Rav Yehuda's reason "mar bar Rav," for he cited the word "there" upon it. Therefore it seems to me Hagahot Maimoniyot holds that not because marriage does not apply is it prostitution — in this it is possible he holds either like Tosafot or like Rosh or Ramban regarding pilegesh; but rather Rambam disagrees with Rashi who explains the prohibition because she is thirsty and lest she fornicate. And Rambam could ask on this explanation: if so, what is the refutation "perhaps it speaks of a minor girl"? And further, "he shall not marry a woman" implies even a minor girl. And further, if there is concern she will fornicate even near her maturity, which is a year — there is concern; therefore he explains the reason of the prohibition that he accustoms the evil inclination upon his son — i.e. he arouses the thirst that acts so one who is sated becomes thirsty; and even "he shall not give his daughter to an old man" — certainly the reason is as Rashi explained; nevertheless regarding a woman to a minor, which implies even a minor girl, he explains from that reason. And it is also possible for a minor there is no concern "lest she fornicate" because she is designated for a man; and everything is by way of asmakhta on the chapter "lest she become pregnant"; therefore Rambam divided the two halakhot because of the difference in reasons. And according to Ramban's explanation on the Torah in this chapter, and upon this they answer "near her maturity — this is different," for then he reached the age of the evil inclination and there is mitzvah to prevent him from sinful thoughts. And for Rabbenu Tur it is reasonable Rambam's explanation; therefore his language is that it is like intercourse of prostitution in imagination — certainly actual prostitution does not apply, only because of the aforementioned reason; and also that this intercourse does not lead to a mitzvah matter, he calls it like prostitution. And even though Rambam used the Gemara's language, if the Tur's explanation is after thirteen years and one day like Beit Yosef, it is possible he saw Rambam's explanation to be stringent and explain "near her maturity" specifically after thirteen; and it is also possible like the later authorities — see and consider. And in my view this explanation is compelled from the summary of Piskei HaRosh who wrote: forbidden to marry a woman to his minor son; mitzvah to marry him when he is near becoming adult; and the prohibition of a minor was not mentioned in Rosh except because of "lest she become pregnant." And it is astonishing on Rif that he omitted this statement. The conclusion from Rambam and Tur's words: marriage of a minor, even to a minor girl, is prohibition; unlike Rashi's explanation. And according to Tosafot in chapter haBa al Yevimto, also before the year near her maturity it is prostitution; and so in responsum Rashba siman 483 that Beit Yosef brought siman 43 — even for minor boy and minor girl, and not as Beit Shmuel's wording implies; unlike Rosh's view. And it is proven also from Tosafot's language in chapter haBa al Yevimto that specifically for this matter the Sages enacted marriage for a minor near her maturity that his intercourse not be prostitution because of the mitzvah, and not for another matter to require a get; and so it is explained there accordingly in Mordechai (and siman 43). And unlike Bach; and in responsum Kenesset Yisrael Beit Yosef brought Beit HaEfodi decided like Tosafot from early generations, and people of Jerusalem who married off sons at eight — and I am astonished: if so, even for Tosafot there is difficulty, for they transgressed "lest she become pregnant." And if you say in early generations they were children of elders and this prohibition did not apply — we could also say to understand Beit Yosef according to Rambam's reason that then they were children of complete intellect and were like adults even for kiddushin. And see Tosafot Sanhedrin 69 in the passage beginning "it is known," etc. He also decided according to his explanation to distinguish regarding a father who marries off his son — he is permitted, and he seizes movable property for the ketubah; and his words are very well founded. This is clear in the Tur siman 67: even a minor whom his father married off — she has no ketubah until he matures; and from this mishnah is the main proof of Tosafot, because presumably the mishnah does not speak of prohibited marriage. And what he asked from prostitution because of absence of ketubah — he should have looked in Beit Yosef siman above: he brings this difficulty in the name of Nekudot HaKesef on marriage of a deaf-mute; and wrote: one could say there regarding intelligent people who are fit for ketubah — does it not appear to them as prostitution? But for deaf-mutes who are not fit to write, he was not particular; and even if she is intelligent — this applies also to a minor. And in our time see Beit Shmuel who concludes in the name of Mordechai and Agudah — therefore in any case we do not compel if he does not fulfill peru u-revu, even in Eretz Yisrael. And these words are not understood: what does Eretz Yisrael have to do with Tosafot's mitzvah of peru u-revu? And in Mordechai it is said regarding one who stayed with a woman ten years and did not bear — see and consider.`,

  "4#_": `And similarly regarding a betrothed woman the gloss: "if he does not wish to consummate but only to release" — Beit HaEfodi explained at the beginning: just as for yevamah he did not enact and one can perform yibbum even though he has a wife, so for betrothed he did not enact what he enacted not to divorce against his will; but if he does not wish to consummate but only to release, he may divorce against his will. And accordingly, in any case it is forbidden to marry before he divorces, unless for the reason mentioned at the end of the gloss — that she does not wish to marry him or to be released — then one may be lenient for him to marry a woman; it appears that whoever she wishes to marry and he refuses — it is forbidden to take a wife. And for this it appears the Rama added that one should not interpret "and similarly for betrothed that it is permitted to marry upon her" — however Chacham Tzvi siman 124 understood "and similarly for betrothed" in Shulchan Arukh that even for betrothed it is permitted to marry upon her if he intends to divorce the betrothed; and the Rama's addition is thus: that she should not err that he is permitted to marry another even though he intends to consummate his betrothal; and on the grounds that the second is not forbidden since the first is not yet his wife; and also the first would be permitted to consummate with another after the second's marriage, since with permission she was betrothed to him — he comes to teach it is forbidden. And its explanation is strained. And if according to Darkhei Moshe he wrote after Maharik's words and Maharam's responsum that Rabbenu Gershom did not decree on betrothed like Maharik above — Maharam's language in siman 14 is: it is further correct to say that Rabbenu Gershom enacted not to marry two wives in his actual language that both are married to him, because they were concerned, etc.; but one of them betrothed — as we say, lest she bear from him, etc. — and we find we can say Rabbenu Gershom did not enact here; and do not add to the enactment except in his clear language that one may not marry two women. From this it appears the Rama learned even from Maharik's words that until here he only enacted not to marry upon his married wife; but where he did not marry her and wishes to retract — who will compel him to write a ketubah and commit to the rest, etc.? And since he wrote he only enacted upon his married wife — behold for betrothed he enacted nothing; i.e. neither the enactment of divorce against his will nor the enactment to marry upon her; for otherwise sometimes through one of these enactments he would be compelled to marry the betrothed — and who will compel us, etc.? And so it appears Beit Yosef understood. If so, Chacham Tzvi's explanation is necessarily compelled; therefore he asked from siman 116 to siman 159 seif 5 in the gloss: why should rabbinic zikah be preferable to Torah betrothal? But in my view, even though in Darkhei Moshe it appears thus, nevertheless it seems the end of the gloss teaches according to Beit HaEfodi above, and not as he returned to explain with strain that if she does not wish to accept the get even though she wishes to marry him and he refuses — it is permitted to marry; on the contrary, from the end of the gloss it appears as above; and Beit Shmuel's explanation is more strained — see and consider.`,
});

patchFile(REL.shmuel, "beit-shmuel", {
  "10#א": `**Rabbenu Gershom imposed the cherem, etc.** The reason is explained in Maggid Mishneh on Ketubot — on account of quarreling was the enactment made, and not on account of a trace of prohibition. And Darkhei Moshe wrote: a practical difference from this is where there is a dispute in the enactment — the lenient party does not lose, since there is no concern of prohibition. He also wrote: now, after the year 5000, the time has already passed, and from then on it is only a custom that they practiced to be stringent; and you are not permitted to permit it in their presence; therefore in a case of doubt we establish the law of Torah.`,
});

patchFile(REL.gra, "beur-hagra", {
  "3#ה": `**They did not practice, etc.** Mordechai wrote in chapter haBa al Yevimto: because the halakhah follows Rav there who said "we do not act," etc.; and furthermore Rav and Shmuel explained only in Eretz Yisrael, as written in chapter haBa al Yevimto seif 4:1 "to teach you," etc.; and end of chapter 3 of Bava Batra "from the day the kingdom spread," etc. — the law is, etc.; and upon that sugya we rely that we do not compel, etc. But according to the law, all these reasons are rejected, for we establish like Shmuel as written siman 154; and so too from that passage in haBa al Yevimto the Rosh already rejected it there; and so too that passage at end of chapter 3 of Bava Batra — Tosafot already wrote there "one could say — but it is written peru u-revu, and perhaps regarding those," etc.; and see Arukh there on the Gemara and Tosafot, for all this is also only in the royal decree specifically.`,

  "7#_": `**And he converted — he and they.** For otherwise what does he say, and it is necessary, etc. — behold a gentile inherits his father by Torah law — see there Rashi and Tosafot who strained themselves to explain regarding firstborn status; but according to Rambam's explanation it is fine, for this is what he says, i.e. Rabbi Yose says he did not fulfill peru u-revu — a convert who converted, etc., i.e. the sons who converted and are not his sons; but because of his own conversion — no, since in his gentile state, etc., as Tosafot wrote there in the passage beginning "Rabbi Yose"; and even Rabbi Yose agrees except he argues because of the sons' conversion, as if the sons died — for a minor, etc.; and upon this he says "and it is necessary," etc. — that fundamentally too, etc., i.e. before they converted; even after he converted — unlike inheritance, for a gentile does not inherit the convert, as written in Kiddushin there. And this is what is written there "they converted" — why plural language of life. And the sugya in Bekhorot does not prove Rambam's words, for he asks in Bekhorot 47a "we learned: who," etc.; and if you say — from where do we know the mishnah speaks when they also converted? And so in Choshen Mishpat siman 277 (should read 277 seif 9) he did not write that they converted; and the Gemara there and in Yevamot makes the necessity there; and also it is not mentioned at all in the Gemara except "and he converted"; and also if you say they did not convert — not certainly regarding them; certainly "a convert who converted," etc., as written there "we learned," etc. — "a gentile is different," etc. — it works for Rabbi Yose, etc. — apparently it is straightforward to the Gemara that in this case certainly plural language of lineage is not needed, and this is obvious without any proof; and so is the view of all the poskim, unlike Rambam.`,

  "8#ב": `**And some say, etc.** From what is said in chapter 22 — mishnah unlike Rabbi Yehoshua; and the mishnah speaks of selling a Sefer Torah, as above; and Rabbi Meir — halakhah follows Rabbi Yehoshua; and books of the Rishonim infer from Rif's words that he brings Rabbi Yehoshua's view on the mishnah. And there is no difficulty — mishnah unlike Rabbi Yehoshua — from which we learn he holds Rabbi Yehoshua does not explain the mishnah; and it speaks of a time of need to sell a Sefer Torah. And this is what is written there: practical difference to sell a Sefer Torah, etc., because of Rabbi Yehoshua.`,
});

patchFile(REL.chokhmat, "chokhmat-shlomo", {
  "4#_": `<small>Seif 10</small> **Rabbenu Gershom imposed the cherem, etc.** Note: I saw in Yeshuot Yaakov that he brought Rashba's words in Kiddushin chapter haOmer that from a verse they derive for all kiddushin "an act is effective"; and see in Choshen Mishpat siman 198 in my fourth edition called Mishpat Tzedek what he wrote on this regarding acquisition of land — see there. And it appears proven from Tosafot to the contrary in Temurah (6a) where they asked: why does he not say there is a practical difference between Abaye and Rava regarding one who swore not to divorce, etc.? And according to Rashba's words one could say: since they expound "and she shall go out and be," etc. — if in kiddushin an act is effective, so too in divorce; and what about against his will — he does not hold thus? It is true as their words prove from the Gemara there, for otherwise what does the Gemara ask Rava from one who divorces under duress; and what does it ask — let us say it is different there, for they derive divorce from kiddushin that an act is effective; and necessarily it will be proven that in divorce one should say "an act is not effective," and necessarily proven that also in kiddushin there is what we say "an act is not effective." However, in truth there is no proof from the Gemara there, for it only asks Rava why he is not lashed; but if the get is not void it does not ask at all; and on the contrary, in our words Tosafot's difficulties there are resolved, since divorce does not work to obligate him for married woman, etc.; and according to what is written, it will be a question that certainly regarding the body of divorce we derive from kiddushin that divorce takes effect; only it asks: for Abaye he is not lashed since his act does not work completely, for he must return; but for Rava, even though his act does not work completely, nevertheless he is lashed for transgressing the master's word; and if so, on the contrary from Tosafot's difficulties there will be proof for Rashba. But from Tosafot's words it will be proven he does not hold thus; and if so, this law is in a second dispute, and I have no leisure to expand; perhaps God will grant me at another time to explain more — see and consider well. And behold, in law: if one sends a get to his wife, is he permitted to marry a woman before it is known whether it reached them — the author debated this in a responsum on whether we say the presumption of an agent, etc.; and I too already wrote on this. And behold, now it seemed to me to add a new permission from what I found in responsum Maharam Chaviv laws of agents siman 21: he wishes to say that even according to the one who does not say presumption of an agent, nevertheless if the agent says he will do this thing, then one may rely on him by virtue of "the remnant of Israel will not do iniquity"; and if so, one could say similarly regarding a get: since the custom is and it is explicit in Shulchan Arukh laws of Gittin that we accept kinyan from the agent who resolves in his heart to be an agent — behold there is statement and act by the agent; therefore it works even for one who does not say presumption of agent. And even though for a get the matter is not in the agent's hand lest she not wish to accept it — nevertheless one could say if the woman begins to consent to accept the get, then one could say in her too this presumption that the remnant of Israel will not do iniquity, and she will presumably accept the get. However, in truth Maharam's words are astonishing to me: if according to his view, why does the Gemara ask in Eruvin (32a) for one who does not say presumption of agent — from the braita of one who says to his friend "pick figs for yourself," and the dispute of Rabbi and Rabban Gamliel — what does it ask? Behold there the homeowner begins with him to say "pick figs for yourself"; and if so, one could say there it is different because the remnant of Israel will not do iniquity and will not give him something improper, since then he is the one speaking to him to pick; and what proof is there from this to the law whether the sender speaks to the agent and the agent says nothing? Therefore his words are undecided. And also according to his words there, he himself distinguishes whether it is possible the sender will do it through another or not; and if so, similarly for a get it is possible he will send her a get through another. And what Maharam brought as proof from Pesachim and chapter haZahav, and what he asked from Kiddushin chapter 2 Abaye and Rava — they do not hold this view of remnant of Israel, etc. — it appears a question: the distinction is like what we say in general "perhaps he died — we are not concerned; perhaps he will die — we are concerned"; for regarding the future it is not relevant to establish a presumption; even though from there there is no complete proof, for there he will eventually die — nevertheless as a type of proof, one can distinguish in presumption between whether the doubt is whether it already happened, and whether the doubt is lest it happen afterward. So too here one could say: certainly where the main doubt depends on whether the act already happened or not — since every matter is fit to establish in presumption that the act did not happen, as is known — therefore this is stronger than presumption of remnant of Israel, etc.; therefore we do not rely on it leniently. But where the doubt is lest it happen afterward — here one properly relies on this presumption of remnant of Israel, etc.; and presumably he will do so; therefore a question: there regarding slaughter for one sitting in prison — the doubt is regarding the future whether they will release him afterward — here we rely on remnant of Israel, etc. And similarly in chapter haZahav regarding one who promised to give him a gift that a Levite may make it tithe — behold now he certainly did not give him, only that he relies he will give afterward — here he can rely on this presumption and make it tithe, for he will certainly give him. But for kiddushin where the doubt is whether she already consented or not — here we do not rely on this presumption. And similarly in Eruvin when he said "pick for yourself a basket of figs" at the time he wishes to eat — the doubt is whether he already tithed them — we do not rely on this presumption of remnant of Israel, etc. And this is correct in reasoning, with God's help; and if so, similarly for a get where the doubt is whether he already gave her the get — for as long as he did not give her it is forbidden to marry a woman, even though he will eventually give her — therefore here this presumption of remnant of Israel does not work to permit marrying a woman even though he and the agent explicitly said they will do so. And this is correct in my humble opinion for practical law — see and consider well.`,
});

patchFile(REL.pitchei, "pitchei-teshuva", {
  "3#_": `**For it is like prostitution.** Avnei Nezer in the name of Kenesset Yisrael who distinguishes whether his father marries him off, etc.; and see in Kenesset Yisrael there who explains according to this reasoning that Tosafot and Rashi and Rosh do not disagree at all with Rambam, Tur, and Shulchan Arukh; for in precision Tosafot and Rosh wrote that it is not considered intercourse of prostitution — i.e. from the aspect of intercourse; and there is also mitzvah to marry off one's minor son — i.e. his father. And the mishnah of "one who writes" also speaks of a minor whom his father married off; and so is the language of Rashi and Rosh; and so early generations and people of Jerusalem would marry off their sons — unlike the language of Tur and Shulchan Arukh. But before thirteen he should not marry — i.e. he himself without his father — for it is like prostitution because it is impossible to write a ketubah; and so Rambam's language can be explained. And he further wrote there that specifically if the father seizes movable property it works; but if the father gives a document on her ketubah it is nothing, lest the father die and not leave enough for his sons' ketubah — see there. And see in Sefer Shaar Naftali in this siman who rejected Kenesset Yisrael's words above, because one should compare marriage of a minor to the matter of marriage of a deaf-mute man and woman for whom the Sages enacted kiddushin and did not enact ketubah (as below siman 67) — see there further.`,
});

patchFile(REL.tz, "turei-zahav", {
  "2#_": `One does not sell a Sefer Torah. Included in this is supporting Torah students. And included in marrying a woman is marrying off orphans with his money, because a woman is also commanded to fulfill shevet yetzera — so wrote Magen Avraham in Orach Chaim siman 156 seif 3, unlike Chayei Adam. And other books are also forbidden to sell, as written in Yoreh Deah siman 74. And Beit Shmuel wrote: however, what Shulchan Arukh wrote in seif 67 in the gloss in the name of Hagahot Asheri — "a woman shall not sit without a husband because of suspicion" — implies she is not commanded regarding shevet yetzera; and so appears in Tosafot beginning of Chagigah; also in Tosafot Gittin 41b and Tosafot Bava Batra 13b they did not write that a woman is commanded regarding shevet yetzera, but for one answer alone — end quote. We do not live by his mouth — he abbreviated where he should have expanded; how does it appear thus in the Tosafot he cited? And when we explain Tosafot's explanation, it will not be as his words; for also for the rest of Tosafot's answers it will not appear at all that a woman is not commanded regarding shevet yetzera. And Tosafot in Gittin there 41b in the passage beginning "He did not create it desolate but for shevet yetzera" — why does the verse of peru u-revu not cite? Because if he could fulfill shevet yetzera in any case we would not compel him because of peru u-revu; and here we compel him as I explained according to the flow of the argument — this is the explanation. On that page 41a Tosafot asked in the passage beginning "to marry a maidservant" — "if he can marry a maidservant, behold he does not fulfill peru u-revu, as proven in Yevamot 62 that a slave has no lineage." And one could say: if he could fulfill shevet yetzera in any case, because of the mitzvah of peru u-revu to acknowledge God we would not compel his master, for he is not obligated in it since he is compelled — end quote. At first glance this is his explanation: specifically because he cannot fulfill both shevet yetzera and peru u-revu, and from the aspect of freedom in him he is obligated in both — therefore we compel his master. But if he could fulfill shevet yetzera we would not compel him because of peru u-revu, since he is compelled and not obligated in it. And so they answered here 41b: therefore the verse of peru u-revu cites — we do not compel his master for this to acknowledge; but from the aspect of servitude he is exempt from both. And so for their answer in another manner: or they cite this verse because it is a great mitzvah; and so in chapter 2 of Megillah regarding one who sells a Sefer Torah, etc. — its explanation is also from what is obligatory from the aspect of freedom in him; but from the aspect of servitude he is exempt also from Shabbat. And so understood Maharam of Lublin in tractate Gittin in the passage beginning "He did not create it desolate," etc. — see there. And since a slave is exempt also from Shabbat, a woman too is exempt, for every mitzvah a woman is obligated in, a slave is also obligated in them; and upon this Beit Shmuel also relied. And Levush wrote: Tosafot — a woman is not commanded regarding shevet yetzera; but accordingly Tosafot's words here contradict Tosafot beginning of Chagigah where they wrote a slave is commanded also regarding peru u-revu, only he does not fulfill peru u-revu because he has no lineage — see there. And here it appears even for shevet yetzera he is not obligated. Also Maharsha sensed this, but he only asked: from this Jerusalem Talmud that Tosafot inferred here that a slave is commanded also regarding peru u-revu; and in chapter haSholeach they inferred from that Jerusalem Talmud that it does not apply to a slave except for shevet yetzera. And according to our explanation there will be difficulty: why did they not raise a great difficulty from this — that in tractate haSholeach it appears even for shevet yetzera he is not commanded? And further, if we do not compel his master for peru u-revu alone because he is not obligated since he is compelled — we should also not compel him for shevet yetzera since he is compelled. Rather it appears his explanation is thus: a slave is commanded also regarding peru u-revu; only if he could fulfill shevet yetzera we would not compel his master because of peru u-revu since he is compelled; and the compulsion now does not come from half-freedom, for even before he could not fulfill peru u-revu since he has no lineage. And if we say here we compel his master so the slave will fulfill peru u-revu — we should compel all slaves to fulfill all mitzvot as Tosafot wrote in Bava Batra 13. But shevet yetzera he could fulfill before when he was a slave; and you from half-freedom cannot fulfill also shevet yetzera — therefore we compel his master; and therefore they also did not cite the verse of peru u-revu, because we do not compel his master because of peru u-revu, as written. And also for the second answer that cites this verse because the mitzvah is great — they also hold a slave is commanded regarding peru u-revu; and they did not cite the verse of shevet yetzera except because it is a great mitzvah. And Ribam's answer that Tosafot brought there — he is commanded only regarding shevet yetzera; and what Tosafot wrote "and so it appears somewhat in the Jerusalem Talmud" — they wish to infer from the Jerusalem Talmud according to Ribam's explanation specifically, and not like their prior answers. And in beginning of Chagigah they infer the opposite from this Jerusalem Talmud that he is obligated also regarding peru u-revu — i.e. not from what is explicit in the question is their precision, but from the question itself: the slave asks what he marries on the festival — it appears he establishes that a slave is not commanded regarding peru u-revu; therefore he asks what there is, etc. And according to the two answers above that a slave is also commanded regarding peru u-revu, it would not be a question at all; therefore they strained themselves also after if the question is resolved to say "all the more so he is commanded regarding peru u-revu" — this is because of shevet yetzera; therefore they wrote "and so it appears somewhat," i.e. somewhat it appears thus — according to the question, and not according to how the question is resolved; for according to how it is resolved it appears he is commanded also regarding peru u-revu, only they needed to interpret it refers to shevet yetzera according to Ribam's answer; but according to the prior answers it speaks even of peru u-revu itself — the slave is obligated without any strain at all. And similarly Tosafot inferred also in beginning of Chagigah; and thereby Maharsha's difficulty is resolved. Also what Maharsha asked there on what Tosafot wrote "and furthermore peru u-revu is written regarding all the children of Noah, even Canaan" — this too is astonishing, for the mitzvah of peru u-revu was stated for Israel and not for the children of Noah, as found in chapter four deaths; and he left it undecided. It appears there is a practical difference: what is said there — every mitzvah stated for the children of Noah and not repeated at Sinai for Israel was stated for Israel and not for the children of Noah; and Rashi's explanation: until Sinai they were commanded in it; and since it was not taught again, we learn Israel who entered holiness stood in their prohibitions, but gentiles took from them — if so, a slave who enters holiness like an Israelite woman — whatever a woman is obligated in, a slave is obligated in them — therefore at least what they were commanded before the Torah, more than a woman — they certainly did not take from them since they too entered holiness; therefore slaves are obligated in peru u-revu even though a woman is exempt. And accordingly, also according to the two answers above we can say a woman is commanded regarding shevet yetzera, as Magen Avraham proves in siman 153; and it appears from the Gemara in Megillah where they say one sells a Sefer Torah to marry a woman because it is written "He did not create it desolate," etc. — why not bring the verse of peru u-revu? Rather certainly even for the woman one sells a Sefer Torah to fulfill shevet yetzera. And what Tosafot did not write also according to the two answers — the reason we do not compel his master for a half-maidservant that she too is commanded regarding shevet yetzera: because they were not compelled for this reason, for we can say she is not commanded at all regarding shevet yetzera even though a slave is obligated also regarding peru u-revu, as written. And what they do not bring in Megillah the verse of peru u-revu — because shevet yetzera is a great mitzvah; but according to Ribam's answer it is proven the maidservant too is obligated in shevet yetzera, for according to his view the slave is exempt from peru u-revu and obligated in shevet yetzera; and accordingly the woman too. And we are compelled to say we do not compel because she is not commanded regarding peru u-revu, etc. But in truth, also for the two answers we can say a woman is commanded regarding shevet yetzera; if so, also to marry off an orphan girl one sells a Sefer Torah. And what is written in the gloss "a woman shall not sit without a husband because of suspicion" and did not say because she is commanded regarding shevet yetzera — one could say it speaks even when she had a son or daughter, for in this she is exempt from shevet yetzera as found in the Gemara Yevamot 62a: Rava said, what is Rav Nachman's reason according to Beit Hillel? As it is said "He did not create it desolate," etc. — nevertheless she shall not sit without a husband because of suspicion. And see there in Gittin in Maharam, for he did not aim well in the chapter of Tosafot in the passage beginning "to marry a maidservant," etc. — see there. And see in Maharsha beginning of Chagigah who emends well in the aforementioned Tosafot. And see in Orach Chaim siman 153 seif 10: an individual who has a Sefer Torah and wishes to sell — what is his law.`,

  "8#ב": `Rather one should marry a woman who is not fit to bear children. Ramban in Milchamot Hashem and Nimukei Yosef disagree in chapter haBa al Yevimto on this: if he finds no woman without selling a Sefer Torah — Ramban holds one sells, and it is forbidden to stand without a wife; Nekudot HaKesef holds one does not sell. And Chayei Adam wishes to explain Nekudot HaKesef's words in another manner so he does not disagree at all on this. And Beit Shmuel wrote: Nekudot HaKesef does not hold thus — end quote. And according to what appears, both did not examine Nekudot HaKesef himself at all, only what Beit Yosef quoted in his name; but Nekudot HaKesef himself wrote explicitly regarding Rabbi Meir: "Forbidden to stand without a wife like Rav Nachman, and one does not sell for this a Sefer Torah" — end quote. And Beit Shmuel wrote: and what he brought as proof in Choshen Mishpat from words of Tosafot Chiddushei that Rama brought nearby that it is worse to sit without a wife because of concern for this — one could say regarding selling a Sefer Torah it is different, for one must do a mitzvah with money and not saving from sin — end quote. And his words are astonishing, for certainly saving from sin is more severe, as written in Yoreh Deah siman 156 and Orach Chaim siman 456; and they say in the Gemara Shabbat 4b and in Tosafot there and Eruvin 32b and Bava Batra 12b in Tosefta and in many places: if one does a severe sin even though he did a sin in selling the Sefer Torah — what of it? A person does a light sin, etc. And similarly difficulty on Nekudot HaKesef: he ruled one sells a Sefer Torah because of Rabbi Yehoshua even though it is rabbinic; and for a woman not fit to bear children one does not sell — and the inclination is as written. And further difficulty: why did Nekudot HaKesef double his words and write he does not release from Sefer Torah except for a woman not fit to bear children — behold he already wrote before this that it is forbidden to stand without a wife and one does not sell a Sefer Torah for this? Rather it appears Nekudot HaKesef holds like Rif that one does not sell a Sefer Torah because of Rabbi Yehoshua; and if so, also for a woman not fit to bear children there is no a fortiori argument; now that even what is forbidden to stand without a wife is rabbinic, as Rambam wrote laws of marriage chapter 15 — from what he is obligated by Torah law we do not derive a fortiori from rabbinic to Torah. And what he wrote "and it is reasonable that when fit to bear children one sells a Sefer Torah because of Rabbi Yehoshua" even though it is only a rabbinic mitzvah — this refers to what he wrote first: "even though he fulfilled peru u-revu, forbidden to stand without a wife and one does not sell a Sefer Torah for this" — i.e. even if no woman is available at all, even not fit to bear children, one does not sell a Sefer Torah for this and sits without a wife. And upon this they wrote "it is reasonable when fit to bear children" — and in selling a Sefer Torah he can also fulfill Rabbi Yehoshua; one sells a Sefer Torah because of Rabbi Yehoshua even though rabbinic, since without selling he sits without a wife entirely, and through selling he fulfills also Rabbi Yehoshua. And upon this he wrote the reason he does not release here from Sefer Torah except for a woman not fit to bear children — i.e. he does not release here in the Gemara a Sefer Torah except for a woman not fit to bear children, i.e. that he can take a woman not fit to bear children without selling — he does not sell for children as Rashi and Tosafot explained there; but if he finds no woman at all and through selling a Sefer Torah he can take a woman fit to bear children — the Gemara does not speak of this at all. Therefore he wrote "it is reasonable one sells"; and even though also when he finds no woman not fit to bear children except through selling a Sefer Torah the Gemara also does not speak — nevertheless there is no a fortiori for this alone, and Rabbi Yehoshua alone as written. If so, this law is included in the laws the author brought: if we say one sells a Sefer Torah so one marries a woman even if no woman is available except through selling a Sefer Torah; and if there is no a fortiori in chapter 22 also for this — therefore the author did not bring this law in Shulchan Arukh, for it is heard automatically; unlike Chayei Adam. And thereby also what Beit Shmuel wrote afterward is negated: "it appears according to the author he holds primarily the first view" — no, for Rosh wrote one sells even though he fulfilled peru u-revu; and so is the view of Meor, Ramban, Ritva, and Nekudot HaKesef; and according to what is written, Nekudot HaKesef concluded his words and so ruled Ritva — end quote; and he understood Nekudot HaKesef holds one sells; and if so Ritva too holds thus; and according to what is written, Nekudot HaKesef holds there is no a fortiori; and if so Ritva too holds thus. And what he wrote that Rosh holds one sells — so is Meor's view; Beit Yosef already wrote there is no decision in Rosh's words he brought of Meor's view, but as he wrote lastly. And one could say they are balanced in his view. And what he wrote that Rosh and Meor understood thus from Rif's words is not compelled, as written in Milchamot Hashem — behold they wrote the reason because Rif did not write "sons"; the Gemara's mishnah unlike Rabbi Yehoshua; and if because Ramban wrote therefore Rif did not bring it because it did not help him at all — even so he must bring the mishnah; what is obligatory from Torah and Rabbi Yehoshua who is rabbinic is not worthy in it to reject Meor and Rosh's words. Did not Rif write several statements — Rav Nachman's statement: even though a man has several sons, forbidden, etc.; and it was stated: he had sons in his gentile state, he fulfilled peru u-revu; and further there, etc. — from all of them we hear the obligation is not from Torah when he had sons; and Ramban's difficulty remains in its place: why did Rif write on the mishnah; and what Ramban wrote that one sells a Sefer Torah even though he fulfilled peru u-revu because of shevet yetzera — behold he makes shevet yetzera for him; the Gemara's intent is not so. And in this siman seif 7 I brought the aforementioned Gemara; and what is written on Bach who wrote in the name of Tosafot 61b that he holds there is no a fortiori — he wrote Tosafot wrote thus according to the mishnah, but for Rabbi Yehoshua it is obvious one must sell — end quote. Bach's intent is: since Tosafot wrote "and Rabbi Yehoshua further says" and did not write "Rabbi Yehoshua says" — we learn even what they wrote initially is Rabbi Yehoshua's view; therefore Bach wrote in the name of Rabbi Yehoshua. And in the first reasoning Tosafot wrote — he holds that for Rav Nachman there is not even mitzvah to marry a woman fit to bear children; and if so, Rabbi Yehoshua's dispute speaks only that there is mitzvah to marry fit to bear children; but one does not sell a Sefer Torah for this — a distant dispute we only establish according to Tosafot's second view that Rav Nachman holds there is mitzvah to marry a woman fit to bear children; therefore Rabbi Yehoshua's dispute holds one also sells a Sefer Torah for this. But since Tosafot wrote "and further Rabbi Yehoshua said one could say even if he has sons," etc., and did not write "and Rabbi Yehoshua further said there is mitzvah" — we learn this is not decided among them that there is mitzvah, but one could say thus. And the proof Bach brings from Tosafot's words — and even if Tosafot's initial words were not Rabbi Yehoshua's view, nevertheless we hear from what he says "and further Rabbi Yehoshua said one could say" and did not write "but mitzvah there is" — as written — from this we hear that also for Rabbi Yehoshua the first view of Tosafot is primary.`,

  "8#ג": `**And he fears that if he marries a woman fit to bear children, quarrels will come, etc.** And Maharam Chaviv wrote: the proof that Tosafot Chiddushei brought from this that they marry him off with advice suitable for him and we push aside the mitzvah of yibbum because of quarreling — is a weak proof — end quote. His words are not clear: what he wrote "there is one who says mitzvah of chalitzah precedes" — behold here too there is mishnah exempting completely from peru u-revu when he has sons. And further, even for one who says mitzvah of chalitzah precedes — it is because perhaps he will marry not for the sake of the mitzvah; but otherwise he also holds mitzvah of yibbum precedes. And what he wrote "yibbum and chalitzah depend on him, the Merciful One" — this too is not so; for Tosafot Chiddushei's proofs from marrying him off with advice because of quarreling: even if the Torah gave him permission in what he wishes, nevertheless they do not marry him off with advice to transgress the prior mitzvah; and certainly it does not speak of wicked people that we should say because of this they give him advice lest he not intend for the sake of the mitzvah — he derives it in Yevamot 44 from the verse "and the elders of his city shall call him," etc. Or because of quarreling it is permitted; and similarly here.`,
});

